.TH VTIUM\-CONFIG 1 2026-11-17 "vtium 0.1.0-alpha.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vtium-config \- set up the fonts and settings of vtium, a terminal for the Linux console
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvtium\-config\fR [\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR]...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets up the vtium console's fonts and settings, and can preview each font live on a vtium console.
.PP
With no command, vtium-config prints its usage. To open the full-screen tool, run vtium-config tui.
.PP
The commands from flow to edit change your draft, a copy of vtium.conf in ~/.config/vtium/drafts/NAME.conf; only install writes /etc/vtium/vtium.conf. The font set commands (new-frit, remove-frit) write a draft and print the command that installs it.
.PP
vtium reads \fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf\fR only when it starts on a VT, so a change that install makes takes effect when vtium starts again. install offers to restart vtium on the VTs it runs on. See vtium.conf(5) for the file and its settings.
.PP
\fBvtium config\fR, a command of the console, runs vtium-config with the words that follow it.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS flow
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBflow\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.PP
Shows how the current draft connects the connected displays and input devices: the displays, the display rules, the heads, the looks, the input rules and the input devices. Each device appears under the first rule that matches it. The first line names the draft, says how it differs from the installed vtium.conf, and counts the problems vtium check-config finds. With \-\-json it prints one JSON object whose version field is 1. It exits 0 whenever it prints, whatever problems it lists.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config flow \-\-json
.fi
.RE
.SS diff
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBdiff\fR
.PP
Shows each line the current draft changes from the installed /etc/vtium/vtium.conf, with its line number in the draft. It changes nothing.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config diff
.fi
.RE
.SS install
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBinstall\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
.PP
Checks the current draft with vtium check-config, then installs it with sudo vtium-config apply DIR, which asks for your password (as root, it installs without sudo). It does not install a draft that fails the check (exit 1), or a draft made before the installed file last changed (exit 3). When the check exits 6, because its only warnings are about fonts that a font set needs and this machine lacks, install shows the warnings and installs the draft anyway. It exits 4 when sudo refuses. A draft whose only problem is that it has no complete path, so that no connected keyboard can type into a terminal on a connected display, is the one exception. At a terminal, install asks before it installs that draft. \-\-force installs it without asking. Without a terminal and without \-\-force, install refuses it (exit 1). vtium-config keeps the file it replaces, so restore can bring it back. vtium reads vtium.conf only when it starts on a VT. So after it installs /etc/vtium/vtium.conf, install enables vtium with sudo vtium console enable on each VT a head names where vtium is not enabled, and shows what vtium console says, refusals included. At a terminal, it then asks whether to start vtium on those VTs and restart it where it runs, so the new file applies now. Each session keeps running through a restart, but its screen is cleared. Without a terminal, install prints those commands instead.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config install
.fi
.RE
.SS get
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBget\fR \fIKEY\fR
.PP
Shows KEY, its value at the top of the draft, and whether the value is set in the draft or is the default. It changes nothing.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config get font\-size
.fi
.RE
.SS set
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBset\fR \fIKEY\fR \fIVALUE\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBset\fR \fB\-\-unset\fR \fIKEY\fR
.PP
Changes a setting at the top of the draft, where it applies to every head, and leaves the rest of the file exactly as it was. \-\-unset comments out the line that sets the key. Like every command that changes the draft, it saves the change even when the check finds problems, and prints what the check finds. \-\-dry\-run shows the change without saving it, and \-\-install installs the draft afterwards.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config set font\-size 14
.fi
.RE
.SS revert
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBrevert\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.PP
Replaces the current draft with a copy of the installed vtium.conf, so the draft's changes are lost. When no vtium.conf is installed, the draft becomes a short comment that says what the file is, and sets no setting. The installed file does not change. \-\-dry\-run says how many lines would be lost.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config revert
.fi
.RE
.SS new
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBnew\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.PP
Starts the current draft over: a short comment that says what the file is, version = 1 and one empty look, [look default]. The draft sets no setting, so vtium uses every default. The draft's heads, rules and looks are lost. \-\-dry\-run says how many lines would be lost.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config new
.fi
.RE
.SS rule
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBrule\fR \fBadd\fR [\fB\-\-before\fR \fIN\fR] \fI'KIND\fR \fIMATCH\fR \fB\-\fR> \fITARGET'\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBrule\fR \fBset\fR \fIN\fR \fI'KIND\fR \fIMATCH\fR \fB\-\fR> \fITARGET'\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBrule\fR \fBset\fR \fIN\fR [\fB\-\-selector\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-target\fR \fIT\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBrule\fR \fBmove\fR \fIN\fR \fB\-\-before\fR \fIM\fR | \fB\-\-last\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBrule\fR \fBrm\fR \fIN\fR
.PP
Changes the [rules] section of the draft. A rule's number N is its place in [rules], counted from 1, as vtium config flow shows it. Without \-\-before, rule add places the new rule so that a broader rule does not catch its devices first: a rule for one device goes before the first rule of its kind for a class of devices, and a rule for a class goes before the first * rule of its kind. Each takes \-\-dry\-run and \-\-install.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config rule add 'input path:pci\-0000:15:00.0\-usb\-0:3.3 \-> desk'
.fi
.RE
.SS head
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBhead\fR \fBadd\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-look\fR \fIL\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBhead\fR \fBset\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINEW\fR] [\fB\-\-look\fR \fIL\fR] [\fB\-\-consoles\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-login\fR \fICMD\fR] [\fB\-\-when\-display\-lost\fR \fIV\fR] [\fB\-\-isolated\fR \fByes\fR|\fBno\fR] [\fB\-\-switch\-on\-enter\fR \fByes\fR|\fBno\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBhead\fR \fBrm\fR \fINAME\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBhead\fR \fBactive\fR \fINAME\fR
.PP
Adds, changes or deletes a [head NAME] section in the draft. A head is a screen: one or more displays that show the same consoles. \-\-name renames the head and every rule that names it. head rm keeps the rules that send to the head; the check reports them until you change or remove each one. head active does not work yet (vtium has no way to receive the request), and exits 2. Each change takes \-\-dry\-run and \-\-install.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config head set desk \-\-look big
.fi
.RE
.SS look
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBlook\fR \fBadd\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-frit\fR \fIF\fR] [\fB\-\-size\fR \fIPT\fR] [\fB\-\-cell\fR \fIWxH\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBlook\fR \fBset\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINEW\fR] [\fB\-\-frit\fR \fIF\fR] [\fB\-\-size\fR \fIPT\fR] [\fB\-\-cell\fR \fIWxH\fR] [\fB\-\-engine\fR \fIE\fR] [\fB\-\-hinting\fR \fIH\fR] [\fB\-\-line\-spacing\fR \fIPCT\fR] [\fB\-\-display\-depth\fR \fID\fR] [\fB\-\-display\-dpi\fR \fIDPI\fR] [\fB\-\-login\-image\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-login\-image\-fit\fR \fIFIT\fR] [\fB\-\-login\-image\-rows\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-login\-image\-position\fR \fIP\fR] [\fB\-\-login\-image\-scale\fR \fIPCT\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBlook\fR \fBrm\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
.PP
Adds, changes or deletes a [look NAME] section in the draft. A look is a font and its size, which heads use. \-\-frit sets its font-name, \-\-size its font-size and \-\-cell its character cell; \-\-engine, \-\-hinting, \-\-line\-spacing and \-\-ligatures set font-engine, font-hinting, font-line-spacing and font-ligatures; \-\-display\-depth, \-\-display\-dpi, \-\-login\-image, \-\-login\-image\-fit, \-\-login\-image\-rows, \-\-login\-image\-position and \-\-login\-image\-scale set the keys of the same name. look add takes the same options as look set. A setting a look leaves out comes from the top of vtium.conf, and in one look a font-size other than 0 overrides cell. look rm does not delete a look a head uses, and names those heads, unless \-\-force switches them to the default look. Each takes \-\-dry\-run and \-\-install.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config look add big \-\-frit monoid \-\-size 14
.fi
.RE
.SS draft
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBdraft\fR \fBlist\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBdraft\fR \fBnew\fR [\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-from\fR \fBinstalled\fR|\fBempty\fR|\fIDRAFT\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBdraft\fR \fBswitch\fR \fINAME\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBdraft\fR \fBrm\fR [\fINAME\fR]
.PP
Manages the drafts in ~/.config/vtium/drafts. A draft is your working copy of vtium.conf. A new draft starts as a copy of the installed file unless \-\-from says otherwise. Without a name it is named after the machine, with \-2, \-3 and so on added so that no draft is overwritten. draft rm without a name deletes the current draft; the next change starts a new one from the installed file.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config draft new laptop \-\-from empty
.fi
.RE
.SS history
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBhistory\fR
.PP
Lists the vtium.conf files that installs replaced, newest first: the number to give restore, the file's name (which names the draft whose install replaced it), and when it was replaced, in UTC. vtium-config keeps the 10 newest; set history = N in ~/.config/vtium/vtium-config.conf to keep another number.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config history
.fi
.RE
.SS restore
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBrestore\fR [\fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.PP
Copies earlier file N (from history; the newest when no number is given) into the current draft. The check runs on it there, and you install it as usual.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config restore 2
.fi
.RE
.SS library
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBlibrary\fR \fBlist\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBlibrary\fR \fBadd\fR \fBlook\fR|\fBhead\fR \fINAME\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBlibrary\fR \fBtake\fR \fBlook\fR|\fBhead\fR \fINAME\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBlibrary\fR \fBrm\fR \fBlook\fR|\fBhead\fR|\fBrule\fR \fINAME\fR
.PP
Keeps looks, heads and rules outside any draft, in ~/.config/vtium/library.conf, written as vtium.conf sections. Adding and taking copy an item; the original stays. Rules in the library, and removing an item, do not work yet; vtium-config says so when you try.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config library add look big
.fi
.RE
.SS edit
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBedit\fR [\fB\-\-install\fR]
.PP
Opens a copy of the current draft in your editor ($VISUAL, else $EDITOR, else vi). When you save and quit, the copy becomes the draft and vtium check-config checks it. If the check finds errors or warnings, vtium-config prints them and asks whether to edit again. When the check exits 6, because it warns only of missing fonts, vtium-config shows the warnings and does not ask. With \-\-install, it then installs the draft, but only if the check exits 0 or 6.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config edit \-\-install
.fi
.RE
.SS fonts
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBfonts\fR
.PP
Lists every font set (frit) and built-in font, one per line: its name, whether vtium can read it, and the font files it needs that are not on this machine. It changes nothing and needs no root.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config fonts
.fi
.RE
.SS check
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBcheck\fR \fIFILE\fR
.PP
Checks FILE as the console would read it. A file named NAME.vtfrit is checked as a font set, and any other file as a vtium.conf, settings and sections together. Each problem prints with the file's name and line number, and a good file prints "ok FILE". It changes nothing and needs no root. It exits 0 when it finds no problem and 1 when it finds one. It exits 6, as vtium check-config does, when a vtium.conf's only problems are fonts that a font set needs and this machine lacks.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config check /etc/vtium/vtium.conf
.fi
.RE
.SS new\-frit
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBnew\-frit\fR \fIFONT\fR... [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-cell\fR \fIWxH\fR] [\fB\-\-size\fR \fIPOINTS\fR] [\fB\-\-layer\fR \fINAME\fR]...
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBnew\-frit\fR \fB\-\-from\fR \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBnew\-frit\fR \fB\-\-template\fR
.br
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBnew\-frit\fR
.PP
Makes a font set (frit) from the fonts, in order: each later font supplies characters the earlier ones lack. A FONT is a family name, or a source as a frit file writes it (kbd:ter-v16n, builtin:unifont). \-\-name names the font set; without it, the name comes from the first font. \-\-cell and \-\-size set the character size. Each \-\-layer adds a layer from the layers folder of the font sets' directory, such as nerd-symbols or noto-emoji. The layers follow the fonts in the order of their roles: scripts, then symbols, then emoji, then the fallback, whatever order you give them in. \-\-from installs a frit file you wrote, and \-\-template prints one to start from. vtium-config checks the font set before it saves anything. Run as a user, it writes a draft and prints the command that installs it.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config new\-frit unifont \-\-name my\-font \-\-size 14
.fi
.RE
.SS tui
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBtui\fR
.PP
Opens the full-screen tool, where you set up the console's displays, keyboards, fonts and settings, and preview fonts live on a vtium console. It runs as you, never as root, asks for sudo when it installs, and needs a terminal on both standard input and standard output.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config tui
.fi
.RE
.SS remove\-frit
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBremove\-frit\fR \fINAME\fR
.PP
Removes the font set NAME.vtfrit. It does not remove a built-in font, a name with no font set, or a font set that font-name names, at the top of vtium.conf or in a look. When the removal is installed, the file is kept as NAME.vtfrit.bak.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config remove\-frit my\-font
.fi
.RE
.SS preview
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBpreview\fR \fIFONT\fR... [\fB\-\-size\fR \fIPOINTS\fR]
.PP
Prints one line for each font: its name, size, character cell, and how many of the preview's characters it can display. In a terminal that supports kitty graphics, an image of the preview follows the line. A FONT is the name of a font set or a built-in font, or a source as new-frit takes it. \-\-size sets the size in points; the default is vtium.conf's font-size, else 12.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config preview unifont \-\-size 16
.fi
.RE
.SS use\-frit
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBuse\-frit\fR \fINAME\fR
.PP
Switches the vtium console you are typing in to the font set or built-in font NAME, until this session ends. vtium-config checks NAME first, then checks that this terminal is a vtium console, and sends nothing otherwise. It fails and says why when NAME is neither a font set nor a built-in font.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config use\-frit unifont
.fi
.RE
.SS apply
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBapply\fR \fIDIR\fR
.PP
Checks the draft in DIR and installs it. A command that changes something writes DIR and prints the sudo command that runs apply on it. apply runs as root.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
sudo vtium\-config apply /home/me/.local/state/vtium/drafts/1
.fi
.RE
.SS serve
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBserve\fR
.PP
Draws a font set's characters for the console: vtium starts it as its font helper. It takes no argument; running it by hand does nothing useful.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config serve
.fi
.RE
.SS report
\fBvtium\-config\fR \fBreport\fR [\fB\-\-save\fR[\fI=PATH\fR]] [\fB\-\-lines\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-previous\-boot\fR]
.PP
Collects what a bug report needs into one plain-text document and prints it: the vtium builds installed and the features each was built with, the kernel and the distribution, the state of each vtium console, the displays the kernel sees, vtium's configuration, and the log of each console. It changes nothing on the machine. \-\-save writes the document to a new file in the current directory, named vtium-report-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.txt (the time in UTC); \-\-save=PATH writes it to PATH. Either prints the file's path and never replaces an existing file. \-\-lines sets how many of each console's latest log lines to include (300 by default). \-\-previous\-boot reads the logs of the boot before this one, for example after a crash that forced a reboot. Run as a user, each part it cannot read says so and names the command that reads it. Run as root (for the kernel's display state and the whole journal), it writes no file and takes no \-\-save, so save its output with sudo vtium-config report > vtium-report.txt. It leaves out the hostname and monitors' serial numbers, but it can still name user accounts (in the logs, in vtium console status and in vtium.conf), and the log times show your time zone. Read the report before you share it.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
vtium\-config report \-\-save
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
The options come before the command. Run as root, vtium-config refuses each option that names a path or a program. As root, it reads and writes only \fB/etc/vtium\fR and its font set directory, and runs only the programs it was built with.
.TP
\fB\-\-configdir\fR \fIDIR\fR
the directory of vtium.conf, instead of /etc/vtium
.TP
\fB\-\-frits\fR \fIDIR\fR
the font set directory, instead of the one font-frits names
.TP
\fB\-\-sudo\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR
the program that runs apply as root, instead of sudo
.TP
\fB\-\-vtium\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR
the vtium binary apply checks files with, instead of /usr/bin/vtium
.TP
\fB\-\-kbd\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
a kbd data directory, instead of the built-in list (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-font\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
an outline font directory, instead of the built-in list (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-vconsole\-conf\fR \fIFILE\fR
vconsole.conf, instead of /etc/vconsole.conf
.TP
\fB\-\-kernel\-cmdline\fR \fIFILE\fR
the kernel command line, instead of /proc/cmdline
.TP
\fB\-\-sys\fR \fIDIR\fR
sysfs, where displays and input devices are read, instead of /sys
.TP
\fB\-\-udev\-data\fR \fIDIR\fR
udev's database, instead of /run/udev/data
.TP
\fB\-\-helper\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR
the font helper to start, instead of this program's serve
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fIDIR\fR
the root directory report reads the machine under, instead of /
.TP
\fB\-\-locale\fR \fILOCALE\fR
the console's locale, which serve picks the built-in font by, instead of C
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR [\fITOPIC\fR]
print the usage, or with TOPIC the help on one command, and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
print the version and exit
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
The command did what you asked.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The command failed. For install, the draft failed vtium check-config. For check, FILE has an error, or a warning about something other than missing fonts.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The command line is wrong.
.TP
\fB3\fR
install did not install the draft, because the installed vtium.conf changed after the draft was made.
.TP
\fB4\fR
sudo refused to run apply.
.TP
\fB5\fR
A file could not be read or written.
.TP
\fB6\fR
check found no problem in FILE but fonts that a font set needs and this machine lacks: a font that is not installed, or one that lacks a character the set checks for. vtium check-config exits 6 in the same case.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBVISUAL\fR, \fBEDITOR\fR
The editor that edit opens, \fBVISUAL\fR first. Without either, edit opens vi.
.TP
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR
The directory that holds \fBvtium/drafts\fR, \fBvtium/library.conf\fR and \fBvtium/vtium\-config.conf\fR, \fB~/.config\fR when unset.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.config/vtium/drafts/NAME.conf\fR
The drafts: your working copies of vtium.conf.
.TP
\fB~/.config/vtium/library.conf\fR
The looks, heads and rules that the library keeps outside any draft.
.TP
\fB~/.config/vtium/vtium\-config.conf\fR
vtium-config's own settings, such as how many earlier vtium.conf files history keeps.
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf\fR
The file that install writes.
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/fonts\fR
The font sets, each in a file \fBNAME.vtfrit\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
vtium(8), vtium.conf(5)
